---
layout: article
title: Single sign-on (SSO)
description: Let your customers' employees sign in to your app through their company's identity provider, such as Okta or Microsoft Entra ID, with OpenID Connect.
---

Single sign-on (SSO) lets the people at your customers' companies sign in to your app with the accounts their company manages in an identity provider, such as Okta, Microsoft Entra ID, Google Workspace, or Keycloak. Each customer is a [team](/docs/products/auth/teams) in your project. A team proves it owns its email domains with a DNS record and connects its own identity providers over OpenID Connect. When someone enters a work email on one of those domains, your app sends them to their company's identity provider. They come back signed in, and Appwrite adds them to the team with the roles you chose.

Use SSO when you sell your app to companies whose IT teams decide who can sign in. A team can also turn on [Require SSO](#require-sso), so its members only get the team's roles after they sign in through the company's identity provider.

SSO is available on Appwrite Cloud with the [Single sign-on add-on](#add-on) for your project.

{% info title="Single sign-on for the Appwrite Console" %}
This page covers SSO for your app's users. To let the members of your Appwrite organization sign in to the Console through your company's identity provider, open your organization's **Settings** and go to **Single sign-on**. Console SSO uses a separate organization add-on.
{% /info %}

# How it works {% #how-it-works %}

1. The user enters their work email in your app. Your app asks Appwrite for the identity providers of the team that verified the email's domain.
2. Your app sends the browser to Appwrite with the chosen identity provider's ID, a success URL, a failure URL, and a random `state` value.
3. Appwrite sends the user on to the identity provider. After they sign in there, the identity provider sends them back to Appwrite.
4. Appwrite checks that the email from the identity provider is on one of the team's verified domains. It finds or creates the user's account and adds the user to the team with the identity provider's roles.
5. The browser lands on your success URL with `userId`, `secret`, and `state`. Your app checks that `state` matches the value it sent, then calls `createSession()` with `userId` and `secret`.

An identity provider only signs in emails on its own team's verified domains. One customer's identity provider can't sign anyone in with an email on another customer's domain.

## SSO and OAuth2 login {% #sso-and-oauth2 %}

[OAuth2 login](/docs/products/auth/oauth2), including its generic OpenID Connect provider, is built for buttons such as "Sign in with Google" that anyone can use. SSO is built for business customers who each bring their own identity provider.

{% table %}
* Feature {% width=200 %}
* OAuth2 login
* SSO
---
* Configuration
* One per provider, for the whole project
* Per team, with any number of identity providers
---
* Who can sign in
* Anyone with an account at the provider
* Only emails on the team's verified domains
---
* Choosing the provider
* The user selects a button
* Appwrite finds the team's identity providers from the user's email
---
* Teams
* No change
* The user joins the team with the identity provider's roles
---
* Enforcement
* None
* [Require SSO](#require-sso) for members on the team's domains
{% /table %}

# Enable the add-on {% #add-on %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Single sign-on add-on card in project settings, with its price and the Enable add-on button](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/add-on.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Single sign-on add-on card in project settings, with its price and the Enable add-on button](/images/docs/auth/sso/add-on.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. In the Appwrite Console, open your project and go to **Settings**.
2. On the **Overview** tab, find the **Single sign-on** card.
3. Click **Enable add-on**.
4. Review the monthly price and the prorated amount due today, then click **Enable**.

If your bank asks for payment authentication, the card shows **Payment pending** until you complete it. Click **Refresh** afterwards to confirm the payment. On a plan that doesn't sell the add-on, the card shows **Upgrade plan** instead.

Once the add-on is active, you can set up SSO on each team's **Single sign-on** tab. Until then, that tab only shows the add-on.

## Pricing {% #pricing %}

The Single sign-on add-on is available on the Pro plan and costs $150 per project per month. When you enable it, Appwrite charges your organization's payment method the prorated amount for the days left in the current billing cycle. After that, the full monthly price is added to each invoice. Prices exclude applicable taxes and fees.

One add-on covers every team in the project. Each project needs its own add-on, and [project environments](/docs/advanced/environments) use the add-on of the project they belong to.

## Disable the add-on {% #disable %}

Click **Disable** on the **Single sign-on** card and confirm with **Disable add-on**. The add-on stays active until the end of the current billing cycle and doesn't renew. Until then, click **Keep add-on** to keep it.

When the add-on ends, nobody can sign in through SSO, and Require SSO stops applying, so members can sign in any way they like. Appwrite keeps each team's domains and identity providers for when you enable the add-on again. Without the add-on, you can still list and delete them and turn off Require SSO, but you can't add, verify, or update anything.

# Set up SSO for a team {% #setup %}

Each team has its own SSO settings. In the Appwrite Console, open your project, go to **Auth** > **Teams**, select the team, and open the **Single sign-on** tab. Setup has three parts:

1. [Verify the team's email domains](#verify-domain).
2. [Connect the team's identity providers](#connect-provider).
3. Optionally, [require SSO](#require-sso) for the team.

Your customer's IT admin controls their DNS and their identity provider, so they add the DNS record and create the application at the identity provider. Send them the values the Console shows.

## Verify a domain {% #verify-domain %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Domains card of a team, with one verified and one unverified domain](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/domains.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Domains card of a team, with one verified and one unverified domain](/images/docs/auth/sso/domains.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. Under **Domains**, click **Add domain**.
2. Enter the domain of your customer's email addresses, such as `example.com`, without the `@`, and click **Add**.
3. Add the TXT record the Console shows at the domain's DNS provider.
4. Click **Verify**.

The TXT record proves that the team owns the domain:

{% table %}
* Type {% width=100 %}
* Name
* Value
---
* `TXT`
* `_<PROJECT_ID>.example.com`
* `team:<TEAM_ID>`
{% /table %}

The Console shows the name relative to the domain's DNS zone, such as `_<PROJECT_ID>`, because most DNS providers add the zone for you.

{% only_dark %}
![The Verify domain dialog with the TXT record to add](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/verify-domain.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Verify domain dialog with the TXT record to add](/images/docs/auth/sso/verify-domain.avif)
{% /only_light %}

DNS changes can take a few minutes to propagate. If verification fails, the Console says whether it found no record or a record with another value. Click **Verify** on the domain's row to check again. Until the domain is verified, nobody can sign in with SSO on it.

- Each domain needs its own entry. Verifying `example.com` doesn't cover `eu.example.com`.
- A domain belongs to one team in a project at a time. Teams in other projects can claim the same domain, because the record name includes the project ID.
- If another team in the project holds the domain, adding it fails with the TXT record your team needs. Add that record and add the domain again. It moves to your team, already verified.
- You can't claim Appwrite's own Functions and Sites domains, or a domain ending on its own, such as `com` or `co.uk`.
- In a [project environment](/docs/advanced/environments), the record name uses the ID of the project the environment belongs to.

## Connect an identity provider {% #connect-provider %}

Appwrite connects to identity providers over OpenID Connect. SAML isn't supported yet.

First, create an application at the identity provider:

1. Create an OpenID Connect web application that uses the authorization code flow and has a client secret.
2. Register this redirect URL. The Console shows it with a copy button when you add the identity provider.

    ```text
    https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/account/sessions/sso/callback/<PROJECT_ID>
    ```

3. Let the application share the user's email address. Appwrite requests the `openid`, `profile`, and `email` scopes, and refuses the sign-in when the identity provider returns no email.
4. Copy the client ID, the client secret, and the discovery URL.

The redirect URL uses the host your app sends the browser to. If your app calls Appwrite through a [custom domain](/docs/products/network/custom-domains), register the redirect URL on that domain, such as `https://api.example.com/v1/account/sessions/sso/callback/<PROJECT_ID>`.

Then add the identity provider in the Console:

1. Under **Identity providers**, click **Add identity provider**.
2. Fill in the settings below.
3. Click **Add**.

{% only_dark %}
![The Add identity provider dialog filled in for Okta, with the redirect URL, roles, client ID, client secret, and discovery URL](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/add-identity-provider.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Add identity provider dialog filled in for Okta, with the redirect URL, roles, client ID, client secret, and discovery URL](/images/docs/auth/sso/add-identity-provider.avif)
{% /only_light %}

{% table %}
* Setting {% width=180 %}
* Description
---
* Name
* Shown in your app when a team has more than one identity provider, such as `Okta`. Up to 128 characters.
---
* Roles
* The team roles users get when they join the team through this identity provider, such as `member`. Add at least one. Roles use letters, digits, periods, hyphens, and underscores, are up to 36 characters long, and can't start with a period, hyphen, or underscore.
---
* Client ID
* The client ID of the application at the identity provider.
---
* Client secret
* The client secret of that application. Appwrite never returns it. When you update the identity provider, leave it empty to keep the stored secret.
---
* Discovery URL
* The identity provider's OpenID Connect discovery document, which ends in `/.well-known/openid-configuration`.
---
* Authorization URL, Token URL, User info URL
* Select **Set the endpoints manually** to override what the discovery document publishes, or to set all three when the identity provider has no discovery document.
---
* Enabled
* Whether users can sign in through this identity provider. On by default.
{% /table %}

Every URL must start with `https://`, because Appwrite sends the client secret and the user's tokens to them.

These are the discovery URLs of common identity providers:

{% table %}
* Identity provider {% width=200 %}
* Discovery URL
---
* Okta
* `https://<OKTA_DOMAIN>/.well-known/openid-configuration`
---
* Microsoft Entra ID
* `https://login.microsoftonline.com/<TENANT_ID>/v2.0/.well-known/openid-configuration`
---
* Google Workspace
* `https://accounts.google.com/.well-known/openid-configuration`
---
* Keycloak
* `https://<KEYCLOAK_HOST>/realms/<REALM>/.well-known/openid-configuration`
---
* Auth0
* `https://<AUTH0_DOMAIN>/.well-known/openid-configuration`
{% /table %}

Google's discovery URL is the same for every Google account. That is safe here, because the identity provider only signs in emails on the team's verified domains.

{% only_dark %}
![The Identity providers card of a team, with an enabled and a disabled identity provider and their roles](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/identity-providers.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Identity providers card of a team, with an enabled and a disabled identity provider and their roles](/images/docs/auth/sso/identity-providers.avif)
{% /only_light %}

A team can have several identity providers, for example one for each subsidiary of a company. Your app then lets the user choose one by name. Each identity provider decides which users can sign in through it. To turn an identity provider on or off, use its switch in the **Enabled** column. To copy its ID, open its actions menu and select **Copy ID**.

## Require SSO {% #require-sso %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Require SSO card of a team, with Require single sign-on turned on](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/require-sso.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Require SSO card of a team, with Require single sign-on turned on](/images/docs/auth/sso/require-sso.avif)
{% /only_light %}

When a team requires SSO, members with an email on the team's verified domains keep their account, but only get the team's roles on a session from one of the team's identity providers. On any other session, such as one from a password, a magic URL, an OAuth2 provider, or a session created before you turned it on:

- Requests run without the roles the team gives, `team:<TEAM_ID>`, `team:<TEAM_ID>/<ROLE>`, and `member:<MEMBERSHIP_ID>`. The member can't reach resources that only the team's [permissions](/docs/advanced/security/permissions) grant.
- Requests to the team's own endpoints, under `/v1/teams/<TEAM_ID>`, fail with `sso_required`.

Nothing else changes for the member. They keep their other teams and the resources their account has access to.

Two kinds of members are exempt:

- Team owners keep their roles however they sign in, so a broken identity provider never locks the team out.
- Members with an email on another domain, such as contractors, keep their roles too, because none of the team's identity providers can sign them in.

To turn it on:

1. On the team's **Single sign-on** tab, find **Require SSO**.
2. Turn on **Require single sign-on** and click **Update**.
3. Click **Require SSO** to confirm.

The team needs a verified domain and an enabled identity provider first. If it later loses either one, or the add-on ends, the Console shows Require SSO as **Paused**, and it applies to nobody until the team has both again.

A session counts as SSO when one of the team's own identity providers issued it. Sessions from an identity provider you disable later still count. Sessions from an identity provider you delete don't.

While Require SSO applies to a member, they can't change their account's email, and `PATCH /v1/account/email` fails with `sso_email_managed`. Otherwise, they could move their account to another domain and get the team's roles back on a password.

# Add SSO to your app {% #sign-in %}

Before you start:

- Add your app as a platform under **Overview** > **Platforms** > **Add platform**. Appwrite only sends users back to success and failure URLs on the hostname of one of your Web platforms, or `localhost`. Native apps return to the `appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>` scheme, which every app in the project can use.
- Appwrite SDKs don't have methods for SSO yet. Call the first two endpoints directly, then use the SDK's `createSession()` to finish.

The **Add sign-in to your app** card on each team's **Single sign-on** tab shows this code for Web, Next.js, SvelteKit, Flutter, React Native, Apple, Android, Python, and HTTP, with your endpoint and project ID filled in. **Copy prompt** copies the same steps as instructions for an AI coding agent.

{% only_dark %}
![The Add sign-in to your app card, with platform tabs, the three steps, and the code for the Web platform](/images/docs/auth/sso/dark/app-sign-in.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Add sign-in to your app card, with platform tabs, the three steps, and the code for the Web platform](/images/docs/auth/sso/app-sign-in.avif)
{% /only_light %}

## Find the identity providers {% #find-providers %}

Send the user's email to `GET /v1/account/sessions/sso/providers`, with your project ID in the `X-Appwrite-Project` header. It needs no session or API key.

```http
GET /v1/account/sessions/sso/providers?email=ada%40example.com HTTP/1.1
Host: <REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
```

The response lists the enabled identity providers of the team that verified the email's domain, oldest first:

```json
{
    "total": 1,
    "providers": [
        { "$id": "<PROVIDER_ID>", "name": "Okta" }
    ]
}
```

- **No identity providers.** The email can't use SSO. Offer your app's other sign-in methods.
- **One identity provider.** Send the user straight to it.
- **Several identity providers.** Let the user choose one by name.

## Send the user to the identity provider {% #redirect %}

Send the browser to `GET /v1/account/tokens/sso` with a redirect or a link. Don't call it with `fetch()`, because the browser has to follow the redirects to the identity provider and back.

{% table %}
* Parameter {% width=140 %}
* Description
---
* `project`
* Your project ID. Required.
---
* `providerId`
* The ID of the identity provider the user chose. Required.
---
* `success`
* The URL Appwrite sends the user to after they sign in, with `userId`, `secret`, and `state` added.
---
* `failure`
* The URL Appwrite sends the user to when the sign-in fails, with `error` and `state` added.
---
* `state`
* A random value your app generates for each sign-in and keeps until the user comes back, for example in a cookie. Appwrite returns it unchanged. Printable ASCII, up to 256 characters.
{% /table %}

The URL looks like this, split over several lines to read:

```text
https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/account/tokens/sso
    ?project=<PROJECT_ID>
    &providerId=<PROVIDER_ID>
    &success=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com%2Fauth%2Fsso%2Fcallback
    &failure=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com%2Fsign-in
    &state=<RANDOM_STATE>
```

Always send `state` and check it when the user comes back. The sign-in doesn't rely on Appwrite's cookies, so your server can start it. That also means `state` is the only thing that ties the result to the browser that started the sign-in.

## Create the session {% #create-session %}

After the user signs in, Appwrite sends them to your success URL:

```text
https://example.com/auth/sso/callback?userId=<USER_ID>&secret=<SECRET>&state=<RANDOM_STATE>
```

Check that `state` matches the value you kept, then delete the kept value. Pass `userId` and `secret` to `createSession()`. The user is already a member of the team at this point, but nobody is signed in until you create the session.

- **In a browser or a native app**, call `createSession()` with the client SDK.
- **In a server-rendered app**, call `createSession()` on your server with an API key that has the `sessions.write` scope. Keep the returned session secret in an `httpOnly` cookie, as in [SSR login](/docs/products/auth/server-side-rendering).

When the sign-in fails, Appwrite sends the user to your failure URL with `state` and an `error` parameter. The `error` parameter holds JSON with `message`, `type`, and `code`:

```text
https://example.com/sign-in?error={"message":"...","type":"user_oauth2_provider_error","code":400}&state=<RANDOM_STATE>
```

Show the message to the user with a way to try again. [Errors](#errors) lists the messages and their causes.

## Platform examples {% #examples %}

Each example covers the three steps. Replace `<REGION>` and `<PROJECT_ID>` with your project's values.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #web title="Web" %}
Install the Web SDK with `npm install appwrite`. Then add the sign-in to `src/lib/sso.ts`. Call `findIdentityProviders()` from your sign-in form, `signInWithSSO()` when the user picks an identity provider, and `finishSSOSignIn()` on the `/auth/sso/success` page.

```ts
import { Account, Client } from 'appwrite';

const ENDPOINT = 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1';
const PROJECT_ID = '<PROJECT_ID>';

const account = new Account(
    new Client().setEndpoint(ENDPOINT).setProject(PROJECT_ID)
);

export type IdentityProvider = { $id: string; name: string };

// 1. Find the identity providers for an email
export async function findIdentityProviders(email: string): Promise<IdentityProvider[]> {
    const url = new URL(`${ENDPOINT}/account/sessions/sso/providers`);
    url.searchParams.set('email', email);
    const response = await fetch(url, {
        headers: { 'X-Appwrite-Project': PROJECT_ID },
    });
    const body = await response.json();
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(body.message);
    return body.providers;
}

// 2. Send the browser to the chosen identity provider
export function signInWithSSO(providerId: string) {
    const state = crypto.randomUUID();
    sessionStorage.setItem('sso-state', state);

    const url = new URL(`${ENDPOINT}/account/tokens/sso`);
    url.searchParams.set('project', PROJECT_ID);
    url.searchParams.set('providerId', providerId);
    url.searchParams.set('success', `${location.origin}/auth/sso/success`);
    url.searchParams.set('failure', `${location.origin}/auth/sso/failure`);
    url.searchParams.set('state', state);
    location.assign(url);
}

// 3. On /auth/sso/success: check the state and create the session
export async function finishSSOSignIn() {
    const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
    const state = sessionStorage.getItem('sso-state');
    sessionStorage.removeItem('sso-state');
    if (!state || params.get('state') !== state) {
        throw new Error('This sign-in was not started here.');
    }

    await account.createSession({
        userId: params.get('userId')!,
        secret: params.get('secret')!,
    });
}

// On /auth/sso/failure: { message, type, code }
export function getSSOError() {
    const error = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('error');
    return error ? JSON.parse(error) : null;
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #nextjs title="Next.js" %}
Install the Node.js SDK with `npm install node-appwrite`, and create an API key with the `sessions.write` scope in `APPWRITE_API_KEY`. The server creates the session and keeps it in its own cookie.

Look up the identity providers from your sign-in form's action, in `lib/sso.ts`:

```ts
export const ENDPOINT = 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1';
export const PROJECT_ID = '<PROJECT_ID>';

export type IdentityProvider = { $id: string; name: string };

export async function findIdentityProviders(email: string): Promise<IdentityProvider[]> {
    const url = new URL(`${ENDPOINT}/account/sessions/sso/providers`);
    url.searchParams.set('email', email);
    const response = await fetch(url, {
        headers: { 'X-Appwrite-Project': PROJECT_ID },
    });
    const body = await response.json();
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(body.message);
    return body.providers;
}
```

Send the browser to the identity provider from `app/auth/sso/route.ts`. Link to `/auth/sso?providerId=<PROVIDER_ID>` from your sign-in page.

```ts
import { cookies } from 'next/headers';
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from 'next/server';
import { ENDPOINT, PROJECT_ID } from '@/lib/sso';

export async function GET(request: NextRequest) {
    const providerId = request.nextUrl.searchParams.get('providerId');
    if (!providerId) return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/sign-in', request.url));

    const state = crypto.randomUUID();
    const jar = await cookies();
    jar.set('sso-state', state, {
        httpOnly: true,
        secure: true,
        sameSite: 'lax',
        maxAge: 600,
        path: '/',
    });

    const url = new URL(`${ENDPOINT}/account/tokens/sso`);
    url.searchParams.set('project', PROJECT_ID);
    url.searchParams.set('providerId', providerId);
    url.searchParams.set('success', new URL('/auth/sso/callback', request.url).href);
    url.searchParams.set('failure', new URL('/sign-in', request.url).href);
    url.searchParams.set('state', state);
    return NextResponse.redirect(url);
}
```

Create the session in `app/auth/sso/callback/route.ts`:

```ts
import { cookies } from 'next/headers';
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from 'next/server';
import { Account, Client } from 'node-appwrite';
import { ENDPOINT, PROJECT_ID } from '@/lib/sso';

export async function GET(request: NextRequest) {
    const params = request.nextUrl.searchParams;
    const jar = await cookies();
    const state = jar.get('sso-state')?.value;
    jar.delete('sso-state');
    if (!state || params.get('state') !== state) {
        return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/sign-in', request.url));
    }

    const admin = new Client()
        .setEndpoint(ENDPOINT)
        .setProject(PROJECT_ID)
        .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!); // Server only
    const session = await new Account(admin).createSession({
        userId: params.get('userId')!,
        secret: params.get('secret')!,
    });

    // On later requests: new Client()...setSession(cookie)
    jar.set(`a_session_${PROJECT_ID}`, session.secret, {
        httpOnly: true,
        secure: true,
        sameSite: 'lax',
        expires: new Date(session.expire),
        path: '/',
    });
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/', request.url));
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #flutter title="Flutter" %}
Install the dependencies with `flutter pub add appwrite flutter_web_auth_2 http`. The sign-in opens in a browser sheet and returns to the `appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>` scheme. Add this to `lib/sso.dart`:

```dart
import 'dart:convert';
import 'dart:math';
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';
import 'package:flutter_web_auth_2/flutter_web_auth_2.dart';
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

const endpoint = 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1';
const projectId = '<PROJECT_ID>';
const callbackScheme = 'appwrite-callback-$projectId';

final account = Account(Client().setEndpoint(endpoint).setProject(projectId));

typedef IdentityProvider = ({String id, String name});

// 1. Find the identity providers for an email
Future<List<IdentityProvider>> findIdentityProviders(String email) async {
  final uri = Uri.parse('$endpoint/account/sessions/sso/providers')
      .replace(queryParameters: {'email': email});
  final response =
      await http.get(uri, headers: {'X-Appwrite-Project': projectId});
  final body = jsonDecode(response.body) as Map<String, dynamic>;
  if (response.statusCode != 200) throw Exception(body['message']);
  return [
    for (final p in body['providers'] as List)
      (id: p[r'$id'] as String, name: p['name'] as String),
  ];
}

// 2. Open the identity provider, and 3. create the session
Future<void> signInWithSSO(String providerId) async {
  final state = base64UrlEncode(
    List.generate(24, (_) => Random.secure().nextInt(256)),
  );
  final uri = Uri.parse('$endpoint/account/tokens/sso').replace(
    queryParameters: {
      'project': projectId,
      'providerId': providerId,
      'success': '$callbackScheme://sso/success',
      'failure': '$callbackScheme://sso/failure',
      'state': state,
    },
  );
  final result = Uri.parse(await FlutterWebAuth2.authenticate(
    url: uri.toString(),
    callbackUrlScheme: callbackScheme,
  ));
  if (result.queryParameters['state'] != state) {
    throw Exception('This sign-in was not started here.');
  }

  final error = result.queryParameters['error'];
  if (error != null) {
    throw Exception(jsonDecode(error)['message']); // { message, type, code }
  }

  await account.createSession(
    userId: result.queryParameters['userId']!,
    secret: result.queryParameters['secret']!,
  );
}
```

On Android, add this activity inside the `<application>` tag in `android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`, so `flutter_web_auth_2` receives the callback. iOS needs no configuration.

```xml
<activity
    android:name="com.linusu.flutter_web_auth_2.CallbackActivity"
    android:exported="true">
    <intent-filter android:label="flutter_web_auth_2">
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
        <data android:scheme="appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>" />
    </intent-filter>
</activity>
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #react-native title="React Native" %}
Install the dependencies with `npx expo install react-native-appwrite react-native-url-polyfill expo-web-browser expo-crypto`. Set the URL scheme in `app.json`:

```json
{
    "expo": {
        "scheme": "appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>"
    }
}
```

Add this to `lib/sso.ts`:

```ts
import 'react-native-url-polyfill/auto';
import * as Crypto from 'expo-crypto';
import * as WebBrowser from 'expo-web-browser';
import { Account, Client } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const ENDPOINT = 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1';
const PROJECT_ID = '<PROJECT_ID>';
const CALLBACK = `appwrite-callback-${PROJECT_ID}://sso`;

const account = new Account(
    new Client()
        .setEndpoint(ENDPOINT)
        .setProject(PROJECT_ID)
        .setPlatform('com.example.app') // Your bundle ID or package name
);

export type IdentityProvider = { $id: string; name: string };

// 1. Find the identity providers for an email
export async function findIdentityProviders(email: string): Promise<IdentityProvider[]> {
    const url = new URL(`${ENDPOINT}/account/sessions/sso/providers`);
    url.searchParams.set('email', email);
    const response = await fetch(url.href, {
        headers: { 'X-Appwrite-Project': PROJECT_ID },
    });
    const body = await response.json();
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(body.message);
    return body.providers;
}

// 2. Open the identity provider, and 3. create the session
export async function signInWithSSO(providerId: string) {
    const state = Crypto.randomUUID();
    const url = new URL(`${ENDPOINT}/account/tokens/sso`);
    url.searchParams.set('project', PROJECT_ID);
    url.searchParams.set('providerId', providerId);
    url.searchParams.set('success', `${CALLBACK}/success`);
    url.searchParams.set('failure', `${CALLBACK}/failure`);
    url.searchParams.set('state', state);

    const result = await WebBrowser.openAuthSessionAsync(url.href, CALLBACK);
    if (result.type !== 'success') return; // The user closed the browser

    const params = new URL(result.url).searchParams;
    if (params.get('state') !== state) {
        throw new Error('This sign-in was not started here.');
    }
    const error = params.get('error');
    if (error) throw new Error(JSON.parse(error).message); // { message, type, code }

    await account.createSession({
        userId: params.get('userId')!,
        secret: params.get('secret')!,
    });
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #apple title="Apple" %}
Add the Apple SDK with Swift Package Manager from `https://github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-apple`. This example needs iOS 16.4 or macOS 13.3 and later. `ASWebAuthenticationSession` catches the `appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>` scheme itself, so there is nothing to register in `Info.plist`.

Look up the identity providers and build the sign-in URL in `SSO.swift`:

```swift
import Foundation

let endpoint = "https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"
let projectId = "<PROJECT_ID>"
let callbackScheme = "appwrite-callback-\(projectId)"

struct IdentityProvider: Decodable, Identifiable {
    let id: String
    let name: String
    enum CodingKeys: String, CodingKey { case id = "$id", name }
}

// 1. Find the identity providers for an email
func findIdentityProviders(email: String) async throws -> [IdentityProvider] {
    var components = URLComponents(string: "\(endpoint)/account/sessions/sso/providers")!
    components.queryItems = [URLQueryItem(name: "email", value: email)]
    var request = URLRequest(url: components.url!)
    request.setValue(projectId, forHTTPHeaderField: "X-Appwrite-Project")
    let (data, response) = try await URLSession.shared.data(for: request)
    guard (response as? HTTPURLResponse)?.statusCode == 200 else {
        throw URLError(.badServerResponse)
    }
    struct List: Decodable { let providers: [IdentityProvider] }
    return try JSONDecoder().decode(List.self, from: data).providers
}

// 2. The URL that sends the user to the identity provider
func ssoSignInURL(providerId: String, state: String) -> URL {
    var components = URLComponents(string: "\(endpoint)/account/tokens/sso")!
    components.queryItems = [
        URLQueryItem(name: "project", value: projectId),
        URLQueryItem(name: "providerId", value: providerId),
        URLQueryItem(name: "success", value: "\(callbackScheme)://sso/success"),
        URLQueryItem(name: "failure", value: "\(callbackScheme)://sso/failure"),
        URLQueryItem(name: "state", value: state),
    ]
    return components.url!
}
```

Open the identity provider and create the session from a button in `SSOButton.swift`:

```swift
import Appwrite
import AuthenticationServices
import SwiftUI

let account = Account(Client().setEndpoint(endpoint).setProject(projectId))

struct SSOButton: View {
    let provider: IdentityProvider
    @Environment(\.webAuthenticationSession) private var webAuthenticationSession

    var body: some View {
        Button("Continue with \(provider.name)") {
            Task {
                do { try await signIn() } catch { print(error) } // Show the error to the user
            }
        }
    }

    // 3. Check the state and create the session
    func signIn() async throws {
        let state = UUID().uuidString
        let callback = try await webAuthenticationSession.authenticate(
            using: ssoSignInURL(providerId: provider.id, state: state),
            callbackURLScheme: callbackScheme
        )
        let query = URLComponents(url: callback, resolvingAgainstBaseURL: false)?.queryItems ?? []
        func value(_ name: String) -> String? { query.first { $0.name == name }?.value }

        guard value("state") == state else { throw SSOError("This sign-in was not started here.") }
        guard let userId = value("userId"), let secret = value("secret") else {
            // On failure, "error" holds JSON with message, type, and code, with "+" for spaces
            let error = value("error")?.replacingOccurrences(of: "+", with: " ") ?? "{}"
            let json = try? JSONSerialization.jsonObject(with: Data(error.utf8)) as? [String: Any]
            throw SSOError(json?["message"] as? String ?? "Sign-in failed.")
        }
        _ = try await account.createSession(userId: userId, secret: secret)
    }
}

struct SSOError: LocalizedError {
    let errorDescription: String?
    init(_ message: String) { errorDescription = message }
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #android title="Android" %}
Add the Android SDK and `androidx.browser` to `app/build.gradle.kts`. The sign-in opens in a Custom Tab and returns to an activity that handles the `appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>` scheme.

```kotlin
dependencies {
    implementation("io.appwrite:sdk-for-android:<VERSION>")
    implementation("androidx.browser:browser:1.8.0")
}
```

Look up the identity providers and open the Custom Tab in `SSO.kt`:

```kotlin
import android.content.Context
import android.net.Uri
import androidx.browser.customtabs.CustomTabsIntent
import java.net.HttpURLConnection
import java.net.URL
import java.net.URLEncoder
import java.util.UUID
import kotlinx.coroutines.Dispatchers
import kotlinx.coroutines.withContext
import org.json.JSONObject

const val ENDPOINT = "https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"
const val PROJECT_ID = "<PROJECT_ID>"
const val CALLBACK_SCHEME = "appwrite-callback-$PROJECT_ID"

data class IdentityProvider(val id: String, val name: String)

// 1. Find the identity providers for an email
suspend fun findIdentityProviders(email: String): List<IdentityProvider> =
    withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
        val query = URLEncoder.encode(email, "UTF-8")
        val connection = URL("$ENDPOINT/account/sessions/sso/providers?email=$query")
            .openConnection() as HttpURLConnection
        connection.setRequestProperty("X-Appwrite-Project", PROJECT_ID)
        val body = JSONObject(connection.inputStream.bufferedReader().readText())
        val providers = body.getJSONArray("providers")
        List(providers.length()) { i ->
            val p = providers.getJSONObject(i)
            IdentityProvider(p.getString("\$id"), p.getString("name"))
        }
    }

// 2. Send the user to the identity provider
fun openSSOSignIn(context: Context, providerId: String) {
    val state = UUID.randomUUID().toString()
    context.getSharedPreferences("sso", Context.MODE_PRIVATE)
        .edit().putString("state", state).apply()

    val uri = Uri.parse("$ENDPOINT/account/tokens/sso").buildUpon()
        .appendQueryParameter("project", PROJECT_ID)
        .appendQueryParameter("providerId", providerId)
        .appendQueryParameter("success", "$CALLBACK_SCHEME://sso/success")
        .appendQueryParameter("failure", "$CALLBACK_SCHEME://sso/failure")
        .appendQueryParameter("state", state)
        .build()
    CustomTabsIntent.Builder().build().launchUrl(context, uri)
}
```

Create the session in `SSOCallbackActivity.kt`:

```kotlin
import android.content.Intent
import android.os.Bundle
import androidx.activity.ComponentActivity
import androidx.lifecycle.lifecycleScope
import io.appwrite.Client
import io.appwrite.services.Account
import kotlinx.coroutines.launch
import org.json.JSONObject

// 3. Check the state and create the session
class SSOCallbackActivity : ComponentActivity() {
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        val data = intent.data ?: return finish()
        val prefs = getSharedPreferences("sso", MODE_PRIVATE)
        val state = prefs.getString("state", null)
        prefs.edit().remove("state").apply()

        lifecycleScope.launch {
            // On failure, "error" holds JSON with message, type, and code
            var error = data.getQueryParameter("error")?.let { JSONObject(it).optString("message") }
            val userId = data.getQueryParameter("userId")
            val secret = data.getQueryParameter("secret")
            if (state == null || data.getQueryParameter("state") != state) {
                error = "This sign-in was not started here."
            } else if (userId != null && secret != null) {
                val client = Client(applicationContext)
                    .setEndpoint(ENDPOINT)
                    .setProject(PROJECT_ID)
                runCatching { Account(client).createSession(userId = userId, secret = secret) }
                    .onFailure { error = it.message }
            }
            // Show ssoError, when set, on your sign-in screen
            startActivity(
                Intent(this@SSOCallbackActivity, MainActivity::class.java)
                    .addFlags(Intent.FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TOP)
                    .putExtra("ssoError", error),
            )
            finish()
        }
    }
}
```

Register the activity inside the `<application>` tag in `AndroidManifest.xml`. If your app already handles the `appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>` scheme for OAuth2, keep the `sso` host on this activity only, so Android doesn't show a chooser.

```xml
<activity
    android:name=".SSOCallbackActivity"
    android:exported="true"
    android:launchMode="singleTask">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
        <data android:scheme="appwrite-callback-<PROJECT_ID>" android:host="sso" />
    </intent-filter>
</activity>
```
{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

# Accounts and team membership {% #accounts %}

The first time someone signs in through an identity provider, Appwrite links them to an account the same way as an [OAuth2](/docs/products/auth/oauth2) sign-in:

1. An account already linked to the user's identity at this identity provider signs in.
2. Otherwise, an account with the same email address is linked. The team proved it owns the domain, so Appwrite trusts the identity provider for the address and marks it verified, even if the identity provider doesn't send `email_verified`.
3. Otherwise, Appwrite creates an account with the user's name and email address.

If the account with the matching email address never verified it, Appwrite treats the SSO user as the address's owner. It signs that account out everywhere and removes its password, phone number, MFA, and other identities before linking it.

Each linked account gets an [identity](/docs/products/auth/identities) with the provider `sso`, which you can see on the user's **Identities** tab under **Auth** > **Users**. Its provider UID is the identity provider's ID and the user's ID at the identity provider, in the form `<PROVIDER_ID>:<SUBJECT>`. New accounts follow your [email policies](/docs/products/auth/email-policies) and your project's user limit, and blocked users can't sign in.

When the user signs in, Appwrite adds them to the identity provider's team:

- A user who isn't a member joins the team with the identity provider's roles.
- A member keeps the roles they have. Changing an identity provider's roles only affects users who join after the change.
- A user with a pending invitation to the team accepts it, and gets the roles from the invitation.

Every user who joins through an identity provider gets the same roles. Appwrite doesn't read roles or groups from the identity provider. To give different roles to different groups of people, connect an identity provider for each group, or change the roles on the [membership](/docs/products/auth/teams) after the user joins.

Removing a user at the identity provider stops their next SSO sign-in, but doesn't end their Appwrite sessions or remove them from the team. To remove their access right away, delete their membership, or delete their sessions or block the user under **Auth** > **Users**. Deleting an identity provider stops sign-ins through it, and the members who joined through it stay in the team.

# Manage SSO with the API {% #api %}

You can manage a team's domains, identity providers, and Require SSO through the REST API with an API key. Read requests need the `teams.read` scope, and changes need `teams.write`. Only the Console, API keys, and the team's owners can manage a team's SSO. Appwrite SDKs don't have methods for these endpoints yet.

{% table %}
* Endpoint {% width=380 %}
* Description
---
* `GET /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/domains`
* List the team's domains.
---
* `POST /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/domains`
* Add a domain with the `domain` parameter. The response has the TXT record in `recordName` and `recordValue`.
---
* `GET /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/domains/{domainId}`
* Get a domain and its `status`, `unverified` or `verified`.
---
* `PATCH /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/domains/{domainId}/status`
* Check the domain's TXT record and verify the domain.
---
* `DELETE /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/domains/{domainId}`
* Delete a domain.
---
* `GET /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/providers`
* List the team's identity providers.
---
* `POST /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/providers/oidc`
* Add an OpenID Connect identity provider.
---
* `GET /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/providers/{providerId}`
* Get an identity provider. The response never includes the client secret, only whether one is set, in `oidcClientSecretSet`.
---
* `PUT /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/providers/oidc/{providerId}`
* Replace an identity provider's settings. Send every setting again, and leave out `clientSecret` to keep the stored one.
---
* `DELETE /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/providers/{providerId}`
* Delete an identity provider.
---
* `PATCH /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/required`
* Turn Require SSO on or off with the `required` parameter.
{% /table %}

For example, this request adds an identity provider:

```http
POST /v1/teams/<TEAM_ID>/sso/providers/oidc HTTP/1.1
Host: <REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io
Content-Type: application/json
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

{
    "name": "Okta",
    "roles": ["member"],
    "clientId": "<CLIENT_ID>",
    "clientSecret": "<CLIENT_SECRET>",
    "wellKnownURL": "https://<OKTA_DOMAIN>/.well-known/openid-configuration",
    "enabled": true
}
```

Instead of `wellKnownURL`, you can send all three of `authorizationURL`, `tokenURL`, and `userInfoURL`. Every change to a team's SSO settings triggers the `teams.<TEAM_ID>.update` [event](/docs/apis/events).

# Rate limits {% #rate-limits %}

{% table %}
* Endpoint {% width=380 %}
* Limit
---
* `GET /v1/account/sessions/sso/providers`
* 60 requests per minute for each IP address.
---
* `GET /v1/account/tokens/sso`
* 50 requests per hour for each IP address.
---
* `POST /v1/teams/{teamId}/sso/domains`
* 10 requests per minute for each user or API key.
{% /table %}

# Errors {% #errors %}

When a sign-in fails, the `error` parameter on your failure URL has one of these types and messages:

{% table %}
* Error {% width=300 %}
* Cause
---
* `user_oauth2_provider_error`: "This identity provider is not available."
* The identity provider is disabled or deleted, the team has no verified domain, or the project's add-on isn't active.
---
* `user_oauth2_provider_error`: "Single sign-on for your organization is not working right now. Contact your administrator."
* Appwrite couldn't reach the identity provider or read its discovery document, one of its endpoints doesn't use `https://`, or it sent an answer Appwrite couldn't read. Check the identity provider's settings.
---
* `user_oauth2_provider_error`: "Single sign-on could not finish. Start again from the sign-in page."
* Someone disabled or deleted the identity provider, or deleted its team's verified domains, while the user was signing in.
---
* `user_oauth2_provider_failure` with `email_missing`
* The identity provider didn't share the user's email address. Let the application release the `email` claim, and check that the user has an email address at the identity provider.
---
* `user_oauth2_provider_failure` with `email_domain_not_verified`
* The user's email at the identity provider isn't on one of the team's verified domains.
---
* `user_blocked`
* The account is blocked.
---
* `user_count_exceeded`
* The sign-in would create an account, and the project already has as many users as its user limit allows.
---
* `user_email_disposable`, `user_email_free`, `user_email_not_corporate`, `user_email_not_canonical`
* The user's email address breaks one of your [email policies](/docs/products/auth/email-policies).
{% /table %}

The other SSO endpoints can return these errors:

{% table %}
* Error {% width=300 %}
* Cause
---
* `sso_addon_required`
* The project's Single sign-on add-on isn't active, so you can't add or verify a domain, add or update an identity provider, or turn on Require SSO.
---
* `sso_required`
* The team requires SSO, and the request came from a member on one of its domains with a session from another sign-in method.
---
* `sso_required_unavailable`
* You tried to turn on Require SSO for a team without a verified domain and an enabled identity provider.
---
* `sso_email_managed`
* A member tried to change their email while their team requires SSO for their domain.
---
* `rule_claim_required`
* Another team in the project holds the domain. The message names the TXT record that moves it to your team.
---
* `rule_already_exists`
* The team has already claimed the domain.
---
* `rule_verification_failed`
* The TXT record is missing or holds another value. The message says what Appwrite found.
---
* `user_unauthorized`
* The user isn't an owner of the team, so they can't manage its SSO settings.
---
* `general_argument_invalid`
* A setting is invalid, such as a domain Appwrite owns, a domain ending on its own, or a URL that doesn't use `https://`.
{% /table %}
